<h2>What the court held</h2>
<p>Granting pre-arrest protection, the court recorded three considerations (para 7): the allegation &ldquo;appears to be purely civil in nature&rdquo;; cases are pending between the parties; and the petitioner has no criminal antecedents. Bail was directed on arrest or surrender within six weeks, on a bond of Rs 10,000 with two sureties, before the court at Aurangabad, subject to Section 482(2) BNSS — with the added condition that one bailor be a close family member.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>Why the family-member surety?</strong> Courts sometimes require a near relative to stand surety so that the obligation to appear is anchored within the household — a practical assurance of attendance that goes beyond a professional surety&rsquo;s bond.</p>
</div>

<h2>The civil-colour doctrine in anticipatory bail</h2>
<p>Indian criminal courts have long been alert to the conversion of money disputes into criminal cases as a pressure tactic. At the quashing stage, that concern produces orders ending the prosecution; at the anticipatory bail stage, it produces something more modest but immediate — protection from arrest while the investigation runs its course. The markers the courts look for are visible in this order: a transaction at the root (money paid, land promised), a long gap between the transaction and the criminal allegation, an existing quarrel or cross-case, and an FIR routed through a Section 156(3) complaint rather than a prompt station-house report.</p>
<p>None of this means the complainant&rsquo;s grievance is false. It means the forum is in question. A written acknowledgement of Rs 11 lakh is strong material — in a suit for recovery. Whether the January 2025 incident independently makes out the assault offences is for the investigation and, if charged, the trial.</p>

<h2>Takeaways</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li>For accused persons: assemble the paper trail of the underlying transaction and every pending cross-case before moving under Section 482 BNSS; the civil character of the dispute is the argument, and documents make it.</li>
  <li>For complainants: a criminal case is not a recovery mechanism. Where money is the real grievance, a civil suit — with interim attachment remedies where justified — is the durable route.</li>
  <li>Surrender-window orders (here, six weeks) are strict: the protection operates only if arrest or surrender occurs within the window, with the bond furnished as directed.</li>
</ul>
</div>
